      "content": "{\"title\": \"Daily Grading System\", \"output_language\": \"zh-CN\", \"facts_per_part_limit\": 12, \"source\": {\"id\": \"part1\", \"line_count\": 95, \"text\": \"[1] HAOHSIANG LIAO: Whenever you\\n[2] have interaction with people,\\n[3] you leave impressions.\\n[4] You are evaluated.\\n[5] So that's a very basic,\\n[6] fundamental idea.\\n[7] We want to give evaluation to\\n[8] our students each performance.\\n[9] That means each class.\\n[10] And the second thing\\n[11] is we are convinced\\n[12] that, as long as students\\n[13] are persistent and consistent\\n[14] in their efforts to\\n[15] take this course,\\n[16] they can demonstrate very\\n[17] good skills in Chinese.\\n[18] So because of those two reasons,\\n[19] we adopt a daily grading system\\n[20] here.\\n[21] So usually, after\\n[22] class, we provide\\n[23] feedback to each student's\\n[24] performance in class--\\n[25] including language, including\\n[26] his cultural behavior--\\n[27] so that students know what to\\n[28] improve for the next sessions.\\n[29] We usually do written feedback.\\n[30] So we use an online-- we have\\n[31] a website called Engrade.\\n[32] So besides entering grades,\\n[33] you can also leave comments\\n[34] to each student privately.\\n[35] So each student, as\\n[36] long as they get access\\n[37] into their website\\n[38] to their account,\\n[39] they will read the comments\\n[40] from the instructor.\\n[41] So the comments--\\n[42] it really depends\\n[43] on the student's performance.\\n[44] It can be as simple\\n[45] as, you know, pay\\n[46] attention to the\\n[47] following pronunciations.\\n[48] It can be a little\\n[49] bit more elaborated.\\n[50] For example, think\\n[51] about, how are you\\n[52] going to deal with\\n[53] compliments in Chinese?\\n[54] For example, this is another\\n[55] example I always like to use.\\n[56] In American culture, when\\n[57] you receive a compliment,\\n[58] it's very natural for\\n[59] you to say thank you.\\n[60] For example, your\\n[61] Chinese is very good.\\n[62] You say, thank you.\\n[63] Your English is good.\\n[64] Thank you.\\n[65] However, being humble\\n[66] is the way when\\n[67] you receive a compliment\\n[68] in Chinese culture.\\n[69] You don't accept it\\n[70] by saying thank you.\\n[71] Sometimes you say things,\\n[72] for example, like,\\n[73] there's still a long way for\\n[74] me to go, so it's too far.\\n[75] Oh, I don't know where the\\n[76] compliments come from. [LAUGHS]\\n[77] So yeah, the fundamental\\n[78] idea in Chinese culture\\n[79] about compliments\\n[80] is to remain humble.\\n[81] So if the student's performance\\n[82] in class is from his L1,\\n[83] we will encourage\\n[84] them and give them\\n[85] the feedback by\\n[86] thinking about, how\\n[87] do you deal with compliments\\n[88] in Chinese culture,\\n[89] according to a textbook\\n[90] where everything\\n[91] is based on the materials\\n[92] we cover in class?\\n[93] So students can know\\n[94] this is something\\n[95] that they can work on.\"}}"
